  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic overview of “photoclick” reactions, showing four general ways light can trigger formation of a covalent product between two partners, labeled X and Y. In all cases, illumination initiates a change in one reactant that ultimately allows X and Y to join, yielding a common photoclick product.\n\nThe left side illustrates direct light activation. In Type I, a light-exposed X species reacts with Y. In Type II, light removes a protecting group, shown as PPG, to unmask a reactive X that then couples with Y. These pathways emphasize photoactivation by cleavage or deprotection.\n\nThe right side shows routes that proceed through especially reactive intermediates. In Type III, irradiation converts a precursor into a highly reactive species based on X, which then rapidly adds to Y. In Type IV, light produces an excited or transient short-lived reactive state, indicated by an asterisk, and this state reacts with Y before it decays.\n\nOverall, the diagram compares different photochemical strategies—direct activation, deprotection, reactive intermediate generation, and short-lived excited-state chemistry—that all serve the same purpose: using light to control when and where a click-type bond-forming reaction occurs.",
